{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2194-11-19 04:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning, I'm Dr. Lee. I understand you came in last night with chest pain. Can you tell me more about what happened?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2194-11-19 04:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I woke up at 11 PM with a squeezing pain in the center of my chest. It went into my left shoulder. I’ve had this before—it feels like my heart pain—but nitroglycerin didn’t help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2194-11-19 04:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. You also had some nausea with it, but no sweating?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2194-11-19 04:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Right, nausea yes, but no sweating. And it wasn’t worse with activity. I’ve had rehab before and never felt this during exercise."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2194-11-19 04:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s helpful. We’ve checked your EKG and it looks like your usual pattern, no new changes. Your first troponin was negative, which is reassuring."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2194-11-19 04:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not my heart? But it feels just like before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2194-11-19 04:26:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Because the pain didn’t respond to nitroglycerin and your heart tests are normal so far, we’re thinking it might not be cardiac this time. We’ll repeat the troponin to be sure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2194-11-19 07:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi again. Your repeat troponins came back negative three times, and your EKG hasn’t changed. That makes a heart attack very unlikely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2194-11-19 07:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So what is it then? The pain is still there, though it’s a bit better since the morphine."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2194-11-19 07:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Given that your heart workup is clear, we suspect this might be coming from your esophagus or stomach. We’re going to try a GI cocktail—antacid and a numbing medicine—to see if that helps."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2194-11-19 07:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Like heartburn? But I’ve been on pantoprazole for years."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2194-11-19 07:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, but sometimes reflux can still cause pain even with medication. The GI cocktail is stronger and acts faster. We’ll see if it gives you relief."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2194-11-19 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see you’re feeling much better now. Did the GI cocktail help?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2194-11-19 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, actually. Within 15 minutes, the pain started fading. It’s almost gone now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2194-11-19 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a good sign. It supports that this was likely a gastrointestinal issue, not heart-related. Your kidney function is stable, and your transplant team checked you and said no changes are needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2194-11-19 12:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re planning to discharge you today. We’ll update your medications—stop the ondansetron because it can affect your heart rhythm, especially with your QT of 485."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2194-11-19 12:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been taking that for nausea. What should I use instead?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2194-11-19 12:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re switching you to Maalox as needed for nausea and indigestion. Also, take famotidine only when you have symptoms, not daily. We want you to follow up with your PCP, cardiologist, and gastroenterologist."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2194-11-19 12:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. So no more heart scare, but I need to check my stomach?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2194-11-19 12:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. Your heart is stable, but we want to make sure we find the cause of this pain. The GI follow-up is important to rule out things like esophagitis or ulcers."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2194-11-19 14:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your discharge orders are in. You’re doing well—pain-free, walking, and labs are stable. Any last questions before we wrap up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2194-11-19 14:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Just to confirm—I keep taking my heart and transplant meds as before?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2194-11-19 14:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, all your usual medications continue: aspirin, clopidogrel, tacrolimus, sirolimus, atorvastatin, and the rest. Only changes are stopping ondansetron and adjusting famotidine."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2194-11-19 14:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And I can use the Maalox whenever I feel nauseous?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2194-11-19 14:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, up to four times a day as needed. And use the nitroglycerin only if you have chest pain that feels truly cardiac—pressure, exertion-related, radiating."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2194-11-19 16:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The transport team will take you home in about 30 minutes. You’re discharged in good condition."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2194-11-19 16:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, doctor. I feel much better knowing it wasn’t my heart this time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2194-11-19 16:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Take care, and don’t hesitate to call if the pain returns or if you have concerns."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2194-11-19 04:07:00",
    "admission_end": "2194-11-19 18:30:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient was admitted with acute substernal chest pain radiating to the left shoulder, associated with nausea but not diaphoresis, unrelieved by sublingual nitroglycerin. Initial concern was cardiac origin given her history of CAD and prior NSTEMI, but serial troponins were negative and EKG showed no new changes. Pain improved rapidly after administration of a GI cocktail, suggesting a gastrointestinal etiology. She remained hemodynamically stable throughout the admission, with no renal complications. The ondansetron was discontinued due to QT prolongation risk, and famotidine was changed to as-needed use. Maalox was prescribed for symptomatic relief. She was discharged home with follow-up arranged with her PCP, cardiologist, and gastroenterologist.",
